WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to "DataVis DevTalk."

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.080
I'm your host, Minhaz Kazi.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.450
Today's guest is Marc Soares.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.690
Marc is the manager of analytic
solutions at ClickInsight.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.060
Marc is also a constant
advocate for effective data

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.800
visualization and storytelling.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.762 --> 00:00:20.845
Marc, welcome to the show.

00:00:20.845 --> 00:00:21.620
MARC SOARES: Thanks, Minhaz.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:22.310
It's good to be here.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:24.227
MINHAZ KAZI: So tell the
audience a little bit

00:00:24.227 --> 00:00:25.260
about yourself.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.810
What is your role
at ClickInsight?

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.800
MARC SOARES: I am the manager
of our analytics solutions team

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.280
at ClickInsight.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.920
We are a digital
analytics consultancy.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.620
We are also a Google
Marketing platform partner.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.400
And my team is
responsible for everything

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.400
from implementing
analytics on a website,

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.110
setting up to
Google Tag Manager,

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.180
creating reports and
dashboards, often

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:51.230
with Google Data Studio, all
the way to conducting analysis

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:54.750
and providing insights and
recommendations to our clients.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.890
So you could say we do the full
stack of digital analytics.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.830
MINHAZ KAZI: So you do
a lot of visualizations

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:02.420
with Data Studio.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:03.800
How do you get started with it?

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.230
MARC SOARES: I was
interested in Data Studio

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:07.940
from the very beginning.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.110
I started using it about as
early as it was available.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:15.530
And I think there were
a couple key benefits

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:20.210
that I saw from very early on
that attracted me to the tool.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:23.600
One was the native integration
with Google Analytics.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.380
The other main benefit was
the ease of sharing reports.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:30.980
You can share a
link, and the viewer

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:32.720
can have access to
that report, even

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.760
without logging
in or downloading

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.630
another application.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.020
And so for those two
reasons, I was really

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.420
attracted to Data Studio
in the first place.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.840
And what's kept me
interested is all the updates

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.820
and enhancements that
have been rolled out

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.680
over time to really make it
a more powerful reporting

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:50.180
product.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.640
MINHAZ KAZI: So you started
with Google Analytics data,

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.560
and you've been creating
reports that you

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.500
share with your key
members and your clients.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.160
But recently, you've been using
Data Studio for other purposes

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.010
as well.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:05.870
For example, you make
almost weekly contributions

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:07.730
to the Makeover Monday contest.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:11.360
So how do you get started
with that, the whole practice

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.760
of taking this new
data set every week

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.390
and then creating visualizations
from that data set?

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.740
MARC SOARES: Makeover Monday
is a community project

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.150
where, every week,
there's a new data set,

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.560
and visual posted,
with the idea being

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.020
that participants will
take that same data set

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.720
and create an improved
version of the chart.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.270
I started participating
in Makeover Monday

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:38.510
even before I started
using Google Data Studio.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.900
And it was for the reason of
giving myself an opportunity

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.930
to practice and improve my
data visualization skills.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.370
And by having that different
chart, different topic,

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.410
different type of
data set every week,

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:54.500
it provides a great opportunity
to practice and learn

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:55.340
new skills.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.660
And with Data Studio, by forcing
yourself to be in the tool

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.620
regularly, I can keep
my skills up to date

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.890
and learn some of
the new features

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.150
and enhancements in the tool.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:09.940
MINHAZ KAZI: One of
your visualizations

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:13.320
were also included in
the Makeover Monday book.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.340
What is your process for
creating these visualizations?

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:17.930
How do you start
with a data set?

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:21.320
What is your process for
picking chart types, or forms

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.100
and colors?

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.980
And what would be the
size of everything?

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.230
Where do you start, how do
you go through the process,

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:28.188
and how do you publish?

00:03:28.188 --> 00:03:30.200
MARC SOARES: I think
I have established

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.240
a pretty consistent
process over the time

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.490
that I've been doing
Makeover Monday.

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.120
It always starts with the data.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:40.702
The data set's usually provided
in a spreadsheet or CSV format.

00:03:40.702 --> 00:03:43.160
When I'm using Data Studio,
the first thing I'm going to do

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:46.130
is create a data source,
so that I have access

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.500
to those fields in Data Studio.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:52.030
But before I jump into
creating any charts or visuals,

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:55.310
I like to get to know
the data set a bit more.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:57.680
I take a look at what
fields are available,

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.230
what dimensions and metrics
are in the data set.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:03.350
And that often requires reading
some background material

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:04.850
on where that data comes from.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:07.890
Often, it's part
of a larger report,

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:10.490
or it was published
as part of an article,

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:13.085
or there's some more
description about that data set.

00:04:13.085 --> 00:04:14.960
I think that's always
important to understand

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:17.360
the data before
you start using it,

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.610
to make sure that
whatever interpretations

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:21.470
you're going to make
or however you're

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:24.020
going to tell the story--
that it's consistent with what

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:25.730
the data actually represents.

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:27.590
Once I get to know
the data, I can

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:31.580
go into creating some
charts in Data Studio.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.270
I try to choose the
most appropriate chart

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:38.830
for the message that I
would like to communicate

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:40.250
or the story that
I want to tell.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:45.530
Depending on whether I'm
trying to show all the data

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:48.460
or focus on a
particular aspect of it,

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:50.210
I may make different
decisions about which

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:51.980
charts are most appropriate.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:55.070
That often involves some
experimentation in the tool.

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:58.580
But once I've decided on what
the most appropriate way is

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.770
to visualize the
data, I can then

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:02.690
add to it with
more visual aspects

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:06.770
of colors and fonts and
layout to really make

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:09.540
a more polished final product.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.330
But I think that,
overall, is the process

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.410
that I follow every week
with Makeover Monday.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.423
MINHAZ KAZI: It is
interesting that when

00:05:15.423 --> 00:05:16.840
you're talking
into visualization,

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:18.940
it's not just the chart types.

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.950
You also have the
data, so you have

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:23.020
to have some domain
knowledge about the data.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:26.350
Then there's the
storytelling part--

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.510
what story am I going to
tell with this whole data?

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:32.950
And then building the vis
based on those two principles.

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:34.600
Your approach to
data visualization

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.040
seems to be very unique.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.620
You're using the common
chart types, like bar charts

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.720
and scatter plots,
and you're operating

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:42.700
within the same techniques
and limitations,

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:45.260
but you're coming up with
something very unique.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:46.420
What inspires you?

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.030
How do you get inspiration
for these unique approaches

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:50.080
to data visualization?

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:52.540
MARC SOARES: Yeah, I
think I draw inspiration

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:54.580
from a lot of different sources.

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:57.730
With many of the Makeover
Monday visualizations

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:01.540
that I've created, I do try to
push the boundaries of what's

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:04.990
possible with the tool in
particular, with Data Studio,

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:08.830
and try to show something
that perhaps people

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:11.170
haven't seen before
or they didn't think

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:12.650
was possible with the tool.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:14.540
But at the same
time, I always try

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:18.010
to respect the
data and the story

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.140
that I'm trying to
tell, and make sure

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:24.460
that the message is clear
and that the visual doesn't

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:25.930
get in the way of them.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:29.380
And in that context I
have drawn inspiration

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:33.190
from people like Edward
Tufte and Stephen Few, who

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:38.020
have written a lot about the
field of data visualization,

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.050
and the principles of
communicating clearly

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:42.160
with information.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:46.570
I also draw inspiration
from others that contribute

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:48.662
to the visualization community.

00:06:48.662 --> 00:06:50.620
There are a lot of creative
and talented people

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:54.100
that create and share
data visualizations,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:58.240
and I think I try and pull
bits and pieces from everything

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.430
that I see and apply
that in my own work.

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:02.240
MINHAZ KAZI: Talking
about community,

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:06.880
you have built a number of
community visualizations

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:09.940
and community connectors
for Data Studio.

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:13.052
This community is still
young and growing.

00:07:13.052 --> 00:07:15.010
What do you think about
the developer community

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:17.320
and the prospects
that a new developer

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.337
has in building solutions
with Data Studio?

00:07:19.337 --> 00:07:20.920
MARC SOARES: I've
done a bunch of work

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:24.940
with the community connectors
and visualizations,

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:27.760
creating those custom
components for Data Studio.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:29.980
One thing I should
clarify is that I don't

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:32.210
consider myself a developer.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:35.740
I can certainly code, and I'm
comfortable with JavaScript

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:39.280
and HTML and CSS, but I'm
not a full-time developer,

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:42.220
nor have I ever been
a full-time developer.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:45.820
But I think that speaks to
the ease with which the custom

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:49.030
components for Data
Studio can be created.

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:52.870
It doesn't require
an expert developer

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:55.450
to be able to connect
to data sources

00:07:55.450 --> 00:07:59.290
or create custom
visualizations for Data Studio.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:02.710
You do need a bit of programming
experience and knowledge,

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.500
but I think what's more
important is to understand

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:08.020
the structure of the
data, how it would be

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:10.480
used in a report or dashboard.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.180
And putting that knowledge
together, I think,

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:16.270
gives anyone a
really good ability

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:18.310
to create these
custom components.

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:22.300
Right now the data vis
community around Data Studio

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:24.380
is still growing.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.880
But I think, because of the
framework that's in place,

00:08:27.880 --> 00:08:30.580
it provides a great
opportunity for more developers

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:33.925
to get involved and build
out more custom components.

00:08:33.925 --> 00:08:37.299
MINHAZ KAZI: What would
be your favorite tip

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:39.932
that you would give to our
audience with Data Studio?

00:08:39.932 --> 00:08:43.220
MARC SOARES: That's
a good question.

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.580
At least one of my
favorite tips is something

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:50.650
that I do in my Makeover Monday
visualizations very frequently.

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:55.000
It's a rather simple thing,
but I hide the page boundary.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.780
What it does is creates a
very clean and crisp look

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:00.840
to your visualization.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.840
The background fills the screen.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:04.340
And if you go into
full-screen mode,

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:07.330
then you really have a true
full-screen experience.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:08.830
So it's something
very simple, but I

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:11.470
think it has a great impact
on the final visualization.

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:13.990
MINHAZ KAZI: When would
you use a pie chart?

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:15.340
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:17.290
MARC SOARES: I think
I may only use a pie

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:21.690
chart to explain to you why
you shouldn't use pie charts.

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.800
They're certainly not
my favorite chart type,

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.500
and there are many
reasons why other chart

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:28.300
types are most often better.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:30.010
MINHAZ KAZI: All, right folks.

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:31.240
That's the end of our show.

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.140
Thanks again to
Marc for being here.

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:35.890
If you want to see more
content on Data Studio,

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:38.350
make sure that you subscribe
to the GCP channel.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:40.450
And you can always visit
our developers' site

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:41.910
at developers.google.com.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:45.280
Until the next time, keep
making more visualizations.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:48.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

